WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.818
<v Tutor>Welcome to Part Two Daily.

2
00:00:01.818 --> 00:00:08.556
<v Tutor>It's Wednesday the eighteenth of November, one hundred and twelve days to the written exam, and this is the deep dive.

3
00:00:08.556 --> 00:00:15.829
<v Tutor>Cardiac week continues, and today we go deep on one of the College's favourite questions: what happens after the heart restarts.

4
00:00:15.829 --> 00:00:26.364
<v Tutor>Specifically, how you predict neurological outcome in the patient who is still comatose after an out-of-hospital cardiac arrest, and how you look after the brain while you wait to find out.

5
00:00:26.364 --> 00:00:28.182
<v Tutor>Here is the running order.

6
00:00:28.182 --> 00:00:38.877
<v Tutor>First, a tutorial: the post cardiac arrest syndrome, the 2025 European Resuscitation Council and European Society of Intensive Care Medicine guideline, and every prognostic test with its trap.

7
00:00:38.877 --> 00:00:42.567
<v Tutor>Along the way, the strange career of therapeutic hypothermia.

8
00:00:42.567 --> 00:00:44.599
<v Tutor>Second, three landmark trials.

9
00:00:44.599 --> 00:00:49.840
<v Tutor>Third, exam practice on the 2024 first sitting question, with an examiner and a candidate.

10
00:00:51.240 --> 00:00:52.268
<v Tutor>Picture it.

11
00:00:52.268 --> 00:00:53.513
<v Tutor>It's day three.

12
00:00:53.513 --> 00:01:02.116
<v Tutor>A fifty-eight year old man, out-of-hospital ventricular fibrillation arrest, bystander CPR, return of spontaneous circulation after twenty-two minutes.

13
00:01:02.116 --> 00:01:04.713
<v Tutor>He's been off sedation for twenty hours.

14
00:01:04.713 --> 00:01:06.066
<v Tutor>His pupils react.

15
00:01:06.066 --> 00:01:07.527
<v Tutor>He extends to pain.

16
00:01:07.527 --> 00:01:12.289
<v Tutor>His wife asks you the only question that matters to her: is he going to wake up?

17
00:01:12.289 --> 00:01:16.671
<v Tutor>Everything in today's tutorial is about answering that question honestly.

18
00:01:16.671 --> 00:01:21.920
<v Tutor>Not too early, not on one test, and not in a way that becomes a self-fulfilling prophecy.

19
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:29.672
<v Tutor>Let's start with how the College asks it, because this is one of the most reliably examined themes in the cardiac block.

20
00:01:29.672 --> 00:01:37.484
<v Tutor>Prognostication after cardiac arrest has appeared as a question seven times since 2011, and four of those were from 2021 onward.

21
00:01:37.484 --> 00:01:42.998
<v Tutor>Hypothermia after cardiac arrest has been asked twice more on its own, in 2015 and 2021.

22
00:01:42.998 --> 00:01:45.928
<v Tutor>So, nine questions on one family of themes.

23
00:01:45.928 --> 00:01:47.995
<v Tutor>The forms are worth knowing.

24
00:01:47.995 --> 00:01:58.392
<v Tutor>In the 2021 first sitting, question sixteen read simply: List the clinical signs and tests used for neuro-prognostication after cardiac arrest and discuss their limitations.

25
00:01:58.392 --> 00:01:59.655
<v Tutor>Note the verb.

26
00:01:59.655 --> 00:02:02.930
<v Tutor>That question wanted limitations, not advantages.

27
00:02:02.930 --> 00:02:12.464
<v Tutor>In the 2023 second sitting, question two read: Outline the approach to neurological prognostication of a patient post out-of-hospital cardiac arrest, O H C A.

28
00:02:12.464 --> 00:02:17.577
<v Tutor>Your answer should include the following headings: General principles, two marks.

29
00:02:17.577 --> 00:02:20.276
<v Tutor>Clinical factors, two and a half marks.

30
00:02:20.276 --> 00:02:22.459
<v Tutor>Imaging, two and a half marks.

31
00:02:22.459 --> 00:02:25.101
<v Tutor>Neurophysiological studies, two marks.

32
00:02:25.101 --> 00:02:26.767
<v Tutor>Biomarkers, one mark.

33
00:02:26.767 --> 00:02:33.200
<v Tutor>The 2024 first sitting asked it again with a guidelines heading, and we'll do that one in exam practice.

34
00:02:33.380 --> 00:02:43.200
<v Tutor>And the 2024 second sitting asked a narrower version: the role of early, under twenty-four hours, versus late, over forty-eight hours, CT brain in the same patient group.

35
00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:47.669
<v Tutor>The commentary on the 2023 question noted two ways candidates lost marks.

36
00:02:47.669 --> 00:02:51.862
<v Tutor>Some misunderstood the question and wrote about brain death testing.

37
00:02:51.862 --> 00:02:53.848
<v Tutor>Others gave generic answers.

38
00:02:53.848 --> 00:02:58.372
<v Tutor>Neurophysiology and biomarkers were generally explored only superficially.

39
00:02:58.372 --> 00:03:00.855
<v Tutor>Hold on to those three failure modes.

40
00:03:00.855 --> 00:03:02.620
<v Tutor>They are the whole game.

41
00:03:03.020 --> 00:03:05.546
<v Tutor>Now the framework the examiners reward.

42
00:03:05.546 --> 00:03:08.610
<v Tutor>If you get a headed question, use their headings.

43
00:03:08.610 --> 00:03:16.942
<v Tutor>If you don't, use five of your own: principles, timing and confounders, clinical examination, investigations, and then synthesis and communication.

44
00:03:16.942 --> 00:03:23.930
<v Tutor>Under each investigation, the examiners want three things: what the finding is, when to do it, and what the limitation is.

45
00:03:23.930 --> 00:03:26.080
<v Tutor>That makes this a natural table.

46
00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.272
<v Tutor>Three columns: test, poor-outcome finding with timing, and limitation.

47
00:03:30.272 --> 00:03:37.260
<v Tutor>If you write it as a table, you'll find it very hard to forget the limitation column, and that's where half the marks sit.

48
00:03:37.660 --> 00:03:39.611
<v Tutor>Before the tests, the disease.

49
00:03:39.611 --> 00:03:50.190
<v Tutor>Oh's Intensive Care Manual covers this in chapter thirty, Cardiopulmonary resuscitation, in the post-resuscitation care section, with the coma side in chapter fifty-nine, Disorders of consciousness.

50
00:03:50.190 --> 00:03:53.066
<v Tutor>The post cardiac arrest syndrome has four parts.

51
00:03:53.066 --> 00:03:56.301
<v Tutor>Here's your first chance to retrieve before I tell you.

52
00:03:56.301 --> 00:03:59.845
<v Tutor>Name the four components of the post cardiac arrest syndrome.

53
00:03:59.845 --> 00:04:01.180
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

54
00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.211
<v Tutor>First, post cardiac arrest brain injury.

55
00:04:09.211 --> 00:04:12.444
<v Tutor>Second, post cardiac arrest myocardial dysfunction.

56
00:04:12.444 --> 00:04:22.310
<v Tutor>Third, the systemic ischaemia-reperfusion response, which looks and behaves a lot like sepsis: vasoplegia, capillary leak, raised lactate, and a temperature that drifts up.

57
00:04:22.310 --> 00:04:30.805
<v Tutor>And fourth, the persistent precipitating pathology, whether that's an occluded coronary artery, a pulmonary embolus, or a subarachnoid haemorrhage.

58
00:04:30.805 --> 00:04:35.683
<v Tutor>That fourth one is easy to forget and it's the one that changes management today.

59
00:04:35.683 --> 00:04:39.300
<v Tutor>So it belongs in any answer about the post-arrest patient.

60
00:04:39.700 --> 00:04:44.387
<v Tutor>The brain injury is what kills most of these patients after they reach ICU.

61
00:04:44.387 --> 00:04:46.815
<v Tutor>The mechanism that matters is this.

62
00:04:46.815 --> 00:04:55.454
<v Tutor>The brain has almost no oxygen reserve, so within seconds of circulatory arrest consciousness is lost, and within a few minutes ATP is exhausted.

63
00:04:55.454 --> 00:05:01.496
<v Tutor>The ion pumps fail, neurons depolarise, glutamate floods the synapse, and calcium pours into cells.

64
00:05:01.496 --> 00:05:07.256
<v Tutor>Then reperfusion adds a second hit: free radicals, mitochondrial injury, and patchy no-reflow.

65
00:05:07.256 --> 00:05:09.119
<v Tutor>The injury isn't uniform.

66
00:05:09.119 --> 00:05:11.829
<v Tutor>Some neurons are selectively vulnerable.

67
00:05:11.829 --> 00:05:19.226
<v Tutor>The CA1 region of the hippocampus, the Purkinje cells of the cerebellum, the middle cortical layers, and the basal ganglia.

68
00:05:19.226 --> 00:05:28.148
<v Tutor>That's why a late MRI shows restricted diffusion in cortex and deep grey matter, and why survivors with moderate injury so often have memory problems.

69
00:05:28.148 --> 00:05:31.536
<v Tutor>And importantly, the injury keeps evolving for days.

70
00:05:31.536 --> 00:05:35.771
<v Tutor>Cell death after reperfusion is not finished at the moment of ROSC.

71
00:05:35.771 --> 00:05:42.660
<v Tutor>That's the biological reason we wait before prognosticating, and the reason fever in the first few days does harm.

72
00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:50.750
<v Tutor>Which brings me to a story, because the way we manage temperature after cardiac arrest has had one of the strangest careers in intensive care.

73
00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:59.464
<v Tutor>In the late nineteen fifties, surgeons were already cooling patients for cardiac surgery, and a few small case series tried cooling patients after cardiac arrest.

74
00:05:59.464 --> 00:06:08.999
<v Tutor>Peter Safar, the Austrian-born anaesthetist in the United States who also championed mouth-to-mouth ventilation and the modern ICU, was a strong advocate in the nineteen sixties.

75
00:06:08.999 --> 00:06:14.484
<v Tutor>But deep cooling was hard to deliver and hard to manage, and the idea faded for about thirty years.

76
00:06:14.484 --> 00:06:16.740
<v Tutor>It came back through the laboratory.

77
00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:26.839
<v Tutor>In the late nineteen eighties and nineteen nineties, Safar's group and others showed in dogs that mild hypothermia, only a few degrees, improved neurological outcome after prolonged arrest.

78
00:06:26.839 --> 00:06:28.377
<v Tutor>Mild was the key word.

79
00:06:28.377 --> 00:06:32.119
<v Tutor>You could get the benefit without the worst of the complications.

80
00:06:32.119 --> 00:06:36.938
<v Tutor>Then in February 2002, the New England Journal published two trials in the same issue.

81
00:06:36.938 --> 00:06:45.140
<v Tutor>One was from Melbourne, led by Stephen Bernard: seventy-seven patients, allocated by odd and even days, cooled to thirty-three degrees for twelve hours.

82
00:06:45.320 --> 00:06:50.639
<v Tutor>Good outcome, meaning discharge home or to rehabilitation, was 49 versus 26 percent.

83
00:06:50.639 --> 00:06:55.148
<v Tutor>The other was the European HACA trial, which we'll take apart shortly.

84
00:06:55.148 --> 00:06:59.715
<v Tutor>Within a year, therapeutic hypothermia was in international guidelines.

85
00:06:59.715 --> 00:07:03.241
<v Tutor>An Australian trial had helped change world practice.

86
00:07:03.241 --> 00:07:04.976
<v Tutor>Then the story turned.

87
00:07:04.976 --> 00:07:10.468
<v Tutor>In 2013, the TTM trial found no difference between thirty-three and thirty-six degrees.

88
00:07:10.468 --> 00:07:19.602
<v Tutor>In 2021, TTM2, with about nineteen hundred patients, found no difference between thirty-three degrees and normothermia with active treatment of fever.

89
00:07:19.602 --> 00:07:22.666
<v Tutor>Six-month mortality was 50 versus 48 percent.

90
00:07:22.666 --> 00:07:24.747
<v Tutor>So what was the real lesson?

91
00:07:24.747 --> 00:07:27.060
<v Tutor>Look at the 2002 control groups.

92
00:07:27.060 --> 00:07:31.569
<v Tutor>Their temperature wasn't actively controlled, and many became febrile.

93
00:07:31.569 --> 00:07:38.795
<v Tutor>The likeliest reading of the whole story is that the benefit came mostly from preventing fever, not from cold itself.

94
00:07:38.795 --> 00:07:45.501
<v Tutor>That's exactly why the 2025 guideline talks about temperature control and fever prevention, not hypothermia.

95
00:07:45.501 --> 00:07:55.040
<v Tutor>And it's a sentence worth putting in an answer: the early benefit was probably fever avoidance, because the controls in 2002 were not temperature controlled.

96
00:07:55.440 --> 00:07:57.499
<v Tutor>So, the governing guideline.

97
00:07:57.499 --> 00:08:09.679
<v Tutor>Today's is the European Resuscitation Council and European Society of Intensive Care Medicine Guidelines 2025: Post-Resuscitation Care, first author Jerry Nolan, published in Resuscitation in October 2025.

98
00:08:09.679 --> 00:08:22.946
<v Tutor>Australian practice through the Australian and New Zealand Committee on Resuscitation follows the same international evidence review, but for the exam, naming the 2025 European guideline with its year is the cleanest choice.

99
00:08:22.946 --> 00:08:30.438
<v Tutor>For prognostication specifically, you can also name the Neurocritical Care Society guideline of 2023, first author Rajajee.

100
00:08:30.438 --> 00:08:33.583
<v Tutor>Here are the key recommendations, with numbers.

101
00:08:33.583 --> 00:08:34.441
<v Tutor>Oxygen.

102
00:08:34.441 --> 00:08:41.818
<v Tutor>Titrate to an oxygen saturation of 94 to 98 percent, or a partial pressure of oxygen of 75 to 100 millimetres of mercury.

103
00:08:41.818 --> 00:08:45.134
<v Tutor>Avoid hypoxaemia below 60, and avoid hyperoxaemia.

104
00:08:45.134 --> 00:08:46.450
<v Tutor>Carbon dioxide.

105
00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:49.480
<v Tutor>Normocapnia, 35 to 45 millimetres of mercury.

106
00:08:49.480 --> 00:08:56.171
<v Tutor>Watch it closely if the patient is cooled, because hypocapnia creeps in and causes cerebral vasoconstriction.

107
00:08:56.171 --> 00:08:57.486
<v Tutor>Blood pressure.

108
00:08:57.486 --> 00:09:02.976
<v Tutor>A mean arterial pressure above 60 to 65, or a systolic above 100 millimetres of mercury.

109
00:09:02.976 --> 00:09:04.120
<v Tutor>Temperature.

110
00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:11.299
<v Tutor>Prevent fever, defined as a temperature above 37.5 degrees, for 36 to 72 hours in patients who remain comatose.

111
00:09:11.299 --> 00:09:17.298
<v Tutor>Don't actively rewarm a comatose patient who is mildly hypothermic, between 32 and 36 degrees.

112
00:09:17.298 --> 00:09:22.591
<v Tutor>If you use a device, use one with feedback from continuous temperature monitoring.

113
00:09:22.591 --> 00:09:25.356
<v Tutor>And no routine prehospital cold fluids.

114
00:09:25.356 --> 00:09:26.708
<v Tutor>The coronaries.

115
00:09:26.708 --> 00:09:33.237
<v Tutor>Immediate coronary angiography for clear ST elevation, or a high suspicion of acute coronary occlusion.

116
00:09:33.237 --> 00:09:40.059
<v Tutor>Without ST elevation, delay the catheter laboratory unless the context strongly suggests an occluded vessel.

117
00:09:40.059 --> 00:09:41.059
<v Tutor>Seizures.

118
00:09:41.059 --> 00:09:42.882
<v Tutor>Diagnose them with EEG.

119
00:09:42.882 --> 00:09:47.940
<v Tutor>Treat with levetiracetam or sodium valproate first line, along with sedatives.

120
00:09:47.940 --> 00:09:50.940
<v Tutor>Do not give prophylactic antiseizure drugs.

121
00:09:51.340 --> 00:09:56.739
<v Tutor>Now the prognostication algorithm itself, which is the core of most of these questions.

122
00:09:56.739 --> 00:09:57.989
<v Tutor>Who enters it?

123
00:09:57.989 --> 00:10:08.843
<v Tutor>In the 2025 guideline, a patient who is not awake and obeying commands, meaning a Glasgow Coma Scale motor score below six, at 72 hours or later after ROSC, with confounders excluded.

124
00:10:08.843 --> 00:10:10.207
<v Tutor>Note the change.

125
00:10:10.207 --> 00:10:13.673
<v Tutor>The 2021 version used a motor score of three or less.

126
00:10:13.673 --> 00:10:16.515
<v Tutor>Either way, the timing anchor is 72 hours.

127
00:10:16.515 --> 00:10:20.947
<v Tutor>Poor outcome is likely when two or more of six predictors are present.

128
00:10:20.947 --> 00:10:23.334
<v Tutor>Here's the second retrieval point.

129
00:10:23.334 --> 00:10:25.834
<v Tutor>Before I list them, try it yourself.

130
00:10:25.834 --> 00:10:31.062
<v Tutor>Name the six predictors of poor neurological outcome in the 2025 European algorithm.

131
00:10:31.062 --> 00:10:32.540
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

132
00:10:37.940 --> 00:10:38.668
<v Tutor>One.

133
00:10:38.668 --> 00:10:42.790
<v Tutor>Absent pupillary and corneal reflexes, at 72 hours or later.

134
00:10:42.790 --> 00:10:43.518
<v Tutor>Two.

135
00:10:43.518 --> 00:10:49.216
<v Tutor>Bilaterally absent N20 waves on somatosensory evoked potentials, at 24 hours or later.

136
00:10:49.216 --> 00:10:50.065
<v Tutor>Three.

137
00:10:50.065 --> 00:10:52.915
<v Tutor>A highly malignant EEG, after 24 hours.

138
00:10:52.915 --> 00:10:59.826
<v Tutor>Highly malignant means a suppressed background, with or without periodic discharges, or burst suppression.

139
00:10:59.826 --> 00:11:00.614
<v Tutor>Four.

140
00:11:00.614 --> 00:11:06.252
<v Tutor>Neuron-specific enolase above 60 micrograms per litre at 48 hours, 72 hours, or both.

141
00:11:06.252 --> 00:11:07.041
<v Tutor>Five.

142
00:11:07.041 --> 00:11:09.526
<v Tutor>Status myoclonus within 72 hours.

143
00:11:09.526 --> 00:11:10.254
<v Tutor>Six.

144
00:11:10.254 --> 00:11:13.710
<v Tutor>Diffuse and extensive anoxic injury on CT or MRI.

145
00:11:13.710 --> 00:11:16.377
<v Tutor>And if the picture is indeterminate?

146
00:11:16.377 --> 00:11:18.317
<v Tutor>Then you keep observing.

147
00:11:18.317 --> 00:11:29.048
<v Tutor>The Neurocritical Care Society guideline explicitly supports an extended period of observation for awakening when prognosis is uncertain, if that fits the goals of care.

148
00:11:29.048 --> 00:11:36.020
<v Tutor>Some of these patients wake up late, particularly older patients and those who had a lot of sedative drugs.

149
00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:42.203
<v Tutor>Now let's go test by test, with the limitation for each, because that's where the marks are.

150
00:11:42.203 --> 00:11:44.227
<v Tutor>Clinical examination first.

151
00:11:44.227 --> 00:11:48.160
<v Tutor>The pupillary light reflex is the most robust clinical sign.

152
00:11:48.160 --> 00:11:54.232
<v Tutor>Use automated pupillometry if you have it, because it's quantitative and less observer-dependent.

153
00:11:54.232 --> 00:11:59.437
<v Tutor>Bilaterally absent pupillary reflexes at 72 hours carry a low false-positive rate.

154
00:11:59.437 --> 00:12:04.410
<v Tutor>The corneal reflex is more vulnerable to sedatives and neuromuscular blockade.

155
00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:06.665
<v Tutor>The motor response is the trap.

156
00:12:06.665 --> 00:12:12.911
<v Tutor>A motor score of three or less, extension or no response, has a high false-positive rate on its own.

157
00:12:12.911 --> 00:12:16.381
<v Tutor>That's why it's an entry criterion, not a predictor.

158
00:12:16.381 --> 00:12:20.776
<v Tutor>Use it to decide who needs further testing, never to decide outcome.

159
00:12:20.776 --> 00:12:22.453
<v Tutor>Myoclonus needs care.

160
00:12:22.453 --> 00:12:35.985
<v Tutor>Status myoclonus, meaning continuous, generalised myoclonus persisting for thirty minutes or more within the first 72 hours, is a poor sign, especially when the EEG shows a suppressed or burst-suppression background behind it.

161
00:12:35.985 --> 00:12:44.429
<v Tutor>But don't confuse it with Lance-Adams syndrome, a chronic action myoclonus that appears as the patient wakes up, with preserved cognition.

162
00:12:44.429 --> 00:12:49.460
<v Tutor>So the rule is: record an EEG during the myoclonus, and look at the background.

163
00:12:49.860 --> 00:12:52.029
<v Tutor>Somatosensory evoked potentials.

164
00:12:52.029 --> 00:12:56.746
<v Tutor>You stimulate the median nerve at the wrist and record over the sensory cortex.

165
00:12:56.746 --> 00:12:59.240
<v Tutor>The N20 wave is the cortical response.

166
00:12:59.240 --> 00:13:08.350
<v Tutor>If it's absent on both sides, at 24 hours or later, the false-positive rate for poor outcome is very low, and it's relatively resistant to sedation and cooling.

167
00:13:08.350 --> 00:13:11.224
<v Tutor>That makes it the most specific test we have.

168
00:13:11.224 --> 00:13:12.525
<v Tutor>The limitations.

169
00:13:12.525 --> 00:13:16.104
<v Tutor>Its sensitivity is low, so a present N20 tells you little.

170
00:13:16.104 --> 00:13:23.478
<v Tutor>It's vulnerable to muscle artefact and electrical noise in the ICU, so you may need neuromuscular blockade to get a clean trace.

171
00:13:23.478 --> 00:13:28.900
<v Tutor>It needs expertise to interpret, and it isn't available everywhere, especially out of hours.

172
00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:29.941
<v Tutor>EEG.

173
00:13:29.941 --> 00:13:34.747
<v Tutor>The highly malignant patterns are the suppressed background and burst suppression.

174
00:13:34.747 --> 00:13:41.637
<v Tutor>A continuous, reactive background early on points the other way, towards good recovery, and that's a useful thing to say.

175
00:13:41.637 --> 00:13:46.978
<v Tutor>Limitations: sedatives cause suppression, so you must read it in the context of drug levels.

176
00:13:46.978 --> 00:13:49.862
<v Tutor>Definitions of reactivity aren't standardised.

177
00:13:49.862 --> 00:13:52.532
<v Tutor>And interpretation varies between readers.

178
00:13:52.532 --> 00:13:55.095
<v Tutor>Remember also the role beyond prognosis.

179
00:13:55.095 --> 00:13:58.460
<v Tutor>EEG finds non-convulsive seizures, which are treatable.

180
00:13:58.860 --> 00:14:00.012
<v Tutor>Biomarkers.

181
00:14:00.012 --> 00:14:03.952
<v Tutor>Neuron-specific enolase is released from damaged neurons.

182
00:14:03.952 --> 00:14:12.074
<v Tutor>Above 60 micrograms per litre at 48 or 72 hours predicts poor outcome, and a rising level between 24 and 72 hours adds weight.

183
00:14:12.074 --> 00:14:17.832
<v Tutor>Older thresholds in your reading may differ, and there's no universally agreed cut-off.

184
00:14:17.832 --> 00:14:19.650
<v Tutor>Third retrieval point.

185
00:14:19.650 --> 00:14:25.894
<v Tutor>Neuron-specific enolase can be falsely high for reasons that have nothing to do with the brain.

186
00:14:25.894 --> 00:14:26.924
<v Tutor>Give two.

187
00:14:26.924 --> 00:14:28.500
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

188
00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:35.443
<v Tutor>First, haemolysis.

189
00:14:35.443 --> 00:14:41.498
<v Tutor>Red cells contain enolase, so a haemolysed sample, or haemolysis from ECMO, falsely raises it.

190
00:14:41.498 --> 00:14:45.594
<v Tutor>Many labs report a haemolysis index, and you should check it.

191
00:14:45.594 --> 00:14:50.580
<v Tutor>Second, neuroendocrine tumours and small cell lung cancer, which secrete it.

192
00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:59.365
<v Tutor>So the limitation sentence is: it's a continuous variable without a universal threshold, and it's confounded by haemolysis and some tumours.

193
00:14:59.365 --> 00:15:07.260
<v Tutor>S100B and neurofilament light chain are promising, but they're not in routine clinical use, so mention them only as emerging.

194
00:15:07.660 --> 00:15:08.514
<v Tutor>Imaging.

195
00:15:08.514 --> 00:15:14.757
<v Tutor>On CT, look for loss of grey-white matter differentiation and diffuse cerebral oedema with sulcal effacement.

196
00:15:14.757 --> 00:15:20.201
<v Tutor>The grey-to-white matter density ratio can quantify this, but thresholds vary between centres.

197
00:15:20.201 --> 00:15:24.576
<v Tutor>The best timing is after 24 to 48 hours, because early CT is often normal.

198
00:15:24.576 --> 00:15:27.832
<v Tutor>And a normal early CT doesn't exclude a poor outcome.

199
00:15:27.832 --> 00:15:33.115
<v Tutor>That's precisely what the 2024 second sitting question on early versus late CT was probing.

200
00:15:33.115 --> 00:15:37.384
<v Tutor>An early CT is about finding the cause, like a subarachnoid haemorrhage.

201
00:15:37.384 --> 00:15:39.305
<v Tutor>A late CT is about severity.

202
00:15:39.305 --> 00:15:40.906
<v Tutor>MRI is more sensitive.

203
00:15:40.906 --> 00:15:46.776
<v Tutor>Diffuse restricted diffusion in the cortex and deep grey matter, best seen between days two and seven.

204
00:15:46.776 --> 00:15:53.660
<v Tutor>The limitations: transporting an unstable ventilated patient, variable quantitative thresholds, and limited availability.

205
00:15:54.060 --> 00:15:56.563
<v Tutor>Here's a quick test on the imaging logic.

206
00:15:56.563 --> 00:15:58.810
<v Tutor>A patient is twelve hours post ROSC.

207
00:15:58.810 --> 00:16:01.006
<v Tutor>The CT brain is reported as normal.

208
00:16:01.006 --> 00:16:03.918
<v Tutor>The registrar tells the family that's reassuring.

209
00:16:03.918 --> 00:16:06.012
<v Tutor>What's wrong with that statement?

210
00:16:06.012 --> 00:16:07.340
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

211
00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:21.640
<v Tutor>Grey-white differentiation is often preserved in the first day, even in patients with devastating hypoxic injury, because the cytotoxic oedema hasn't declared itself yet.

212
00:16:21.640 --> 00:16:24.890
<v Tutor>So an early normal CT has poor negative predictive value.

213
00:16:24.890 --> 00:16:26.640
<v Tutor>It rules out a haemorrhage.

214
00:16:26.640 --> 00:16:29.090
<v Tutor>It doesn't rule out hypoxic brain injury.

215
00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:35.540
<v Tutor>The right message to the family is that the scan has excluded a bleed, and that it's too early to judge the brain injury.

216
00:16:35.940 --> 00:16:45.935
<v Tutor>While we wait for 72 hours, the rest of the organ support matters, because the other three parts of the syndrome can kill the patient before the brain declares itself.

217
00:16:45.935 --> 00:16:46.963
<v Tutor>The heart.

218
00:16:46.963 --> 00:16:52.217
<v Tutor>Myocardial stunning after arrest is common and usually improves over 48 to 72 hours.

219
00:16:52.217 --> 00:16:53.702
<v Tutor>Get an early echo.

220
00:16:53.702 --> 00:17:01.526
<v Tutor>Look for a regional wall motion abnormality, right ventricular dilatation suggesting pulmonary embolism, and tamponade after CPR.

221
00:17:01.526 --> 00:17:08.151
<v Tutor>If the patient is in cardiogenic shock, use fluids, then noradrenaline, then an inotrope such as dobutamine.

222
00:17:08.151 --> 00:17:11.749
<v Tutor>Mechanical support is for selected patients after that.

223
00:17:11.749 --> 00:17:12.891
<v Tutor>Ventilation.

224
00:17:12.891 --> 00:17:18.431
<v Tutor>Lung-protective volumes, around 6 to 8 millilitres per kilogram of predicted body weight.

225
00:17:18.431 --> 00:17:23.571
<v Tutor>Remember that aspiration pneumonia is common after arrest, so send cultures early.

226
00:17:23.571 --> 00:17:24.485
<v Tutor>Glucose.

227
00:17:24.485 --> 00:17:27.398
<v Tutor>Avoid both hypoglycaemia and tight control.

228
00:17:27.398 --> 00:17:32.423
<v Tutor>A target of roughly 7.8 to 10 millimoles per litre is a sensible range to quote.

229
00:17:32.423 --> 00:17:33.394
<v Tutor>Sedation.

230
00:17:33.394 --> 00:17:38.363
<v Tutor>Use short-acting agents, because you'll need to clear them for prognostication.

231
00:17:38.363 --> 00:17:43.617
<v Tutor>And treat shivering, which raises metabolic rate and fights your temperature device.

232
00:17:43.617 --> 00:17:49.100
<v Tutor>And one distinction that cost candidates marks in 2023: this is not brain death testing.

233
00:17:49.280 --> 00:17:54.691
<v Tutor>Brain death needs established preconditions, an observation period and absent brainstem function.

234
00:17:54.691 --> 00:18:00.462
<v Tutor>Neuroprognostication is a probabilistic judgement in a patient who usually still has brainstem reflexes.

235
00:18:00.462 --> 00:18:06.440
<v Tutor>If brainstem reflexes are lost and the patient progresses to brain death, that's a separate, formal process.

236
00:18:06.840 --> 00:18:12.246
<v Tutor>Now confounders, because the examiners specifically want them under general principles.

237
00:18:12.246 --> 00:18:17.196
<v Tutor>Sedatives and opioids, especially accumulated propofol, midazolam and fentanyl.

238
00:18:17.196 --> 00:18:24.081
<v Tutor>Their clearance is slowed by hypothermia, and by renal and liver dysfunction, so wait longer after stopping them.

239
00:18:24.081 --> 00:18:27.666
<v Tutor>Use short-acting agents from the start for that reason.

240
00:18:27.666 --> 00:18:29.430
<v Tutor>Neuromuscular blockade.

241
00:18:29.430 --> 00:18:31.934
<v Tutor>Check train-of-four before the exam.

242
00:18:31.934 --> 00:18:34.438
<v Tutor>Hypothermia itself, and hypotension.

243
00:18:34.438 --> 00:18:40.412
<v Tutor>Metabolic causes: hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, uraemia, hepatic encephalopathy, and hypercapnia.

244
00:18:40.412 --> 00:18:44.111
<v Tutor>And non-convulsive seizures, or a new intracranial event.

245
00:18:44.111 --> 00:18:48.720
<v Tutor>If any of these are present, the clock doesn't start until they're fixed.

246
00:18:49.120 --> 00:18:51.456
<v Tutor>Finally, synthesis and communication.

247
00:18:51.456 --> 00:19:00.798
<v Tutor>Most deaths in patients admitted comatose after out-of-hospital cardiac arrest follow withdrawal of life-sustaining therapy, based on a predicted poor neurological outcome.

248
00:19:00.798 --> 00:19:03.497
<v Tutor>That's the self-fulfilling prophecy problem.

249
00:19:03.497 --> 00:19:07.026
<v Tutor>Withdraw too early, and you'll never see the late awakeners.

250
00:19:07.026 --> 00:19:10.607
<v Tutor>Your data then confirm that patients like this don't wake up.

251
00:19:10.607 --> 00:19:19.794
<v Tutor>So the consultant answer says: prognosticate using at least two concordant predictors, at the right time, without confounders, by a senior clinician, documented clearly.

252
00:19:19.794 --> 00:19:23.842
<v Tutor>Have early and honest conversations with the family about uncertainty.

253
00:19:23.842 --> 00:19:30.174
<v Tutor>Consider organ donation pathways where appropriate, and refer to the donation service according to local practice.

254
00:19:30.174 --> 00:19:37.440
<v Tutor>And plan follow-up for survivors, because cognitive and psychological problems after cardiac arrest are common and under-recognised.

255
00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:40.624
<v Tutor>Here are your three numbers to remember.

256
00:19:40.624 --> 00:19:46.888
<v Tutor>Seventy-two hours: the earliest point for the prognostication algorithm, after confounders are gone.

257
00:19:46.888 --> 00:19:53.558
<v Tutor>Sixty micrograms per litre: the neuron-specific enolase threshold at 48 or 72 hours, in the 2025 guideline.

258
00:19:53.558 --> 00:19:59.068
<v Tutor>Thirty-seven point five degrees: the fever threshold to stay below, for 36 to 72 hours.

259
00:19:59.068 --> 00:20:00.286
<v Tutor>And one rule.

260
00:20:00.286 --> 00:20:03.360
<v Tutor>Two or more concordant predictors, never one.

261
00:20:04.760 --> 00:20:06.281
<v Tutor>Now three trials.

262
00:20:06.281 --> 00:20:15.344
<v Tutor>Each one answers a question an examiner could put to you: does cooling help, does adrenaline help, and how high should the blood pressure be.

263
00:20:15.344 --> 00:20:22.644
<v Tutor>We'll start with HACA, the Hypothermia after Cardiac Arrest trial, published in the New England Journal in 2002.

264
00:20:22.644 --> 00:20:26.902
<v Tutor>It's chapter fifty-eight of Landmark Studies in Critical Care.

265
00:20:26.902 --> 00:20:38.581
<v Tutor>The population was tightly selected: adults aged 18 to 75, comatose after a witnessed out-of-hospital arrest with ventricular fibrillation, and return of circulation within 60 minutes.

266
00:20:38.581 --> 00:20:43.569
<v Tutor>It was done in nine centres in five European countries, with 275 patients.

267
00:20:43.569 --> 00:20:54.518
<v Tutor>The intervention was cooling to 32 to 34 degrees bladder temperature for 24 hours, mainly with an external forced-air device, then passive rewarming over about eight hours.

268
00:20:54.518 --> 00:21:00.176
<v Tutor>The comparator was standard care with normothermia, which wasn't actively controlled.

269
00:21:00.176 --> 00:21:09.482
<v Tutor>The primary outcome, a favourable neurological outcome at six months, meaning Cerebral Performance Category one or two, was 55 versus 39 percent.

270
00:21:09.482 --> 00:21:11.125
<v Tutor>Relative risk 1.40.

271
00:21:11.125 --> 00:21:13.315
<v Tutor>Number needed to treat, six.

272
00:21:13.315 --> 00:21:16.539
<v Tutor>Six-month mortality was 41 versus 55 percent.

273
00:21:16.539 --> 00:21:21.040
<v Tutor>On harm, complications were similar overall, 73 versus 70 percent.

274
00:21:21.220 --> 00:21:25.593
<v Tutor>Pneumonia, sepsis and bleeding were numerically higher with cooling.

275
00:21:25.593 --> 00:21:27.205
<v Tutor>The main criticisms.

276
00:21:27.205 --> 00:21:33.649
<v Tutor>It stopped early because enrolment was slow and funding ran out, which tends to exaggerate effect sizes.

277
00:21:33.649 --> 00:21:38.886
<v Tutor>It was open label for the treating team, though the outcome assessors were blinded.

278
00:21:38.886 --> 00:21:42.972
<v Tutor>Cooling was slow, with a median of eight hours to reach target.

279
00:21:42.972 --> 00:21:48.381
<v Tutor>And, most importantly, the control arm wasn't protocolised, so fever wasn't prevented.

280
00:21:48.381 --> 00:21:55.401
<v Tutor>What it changed: with Bernard's trial in the same issue, it put therapeutic hypothermia into guidelines worldwide.

281
00:21:55.401 --> 00:21:57.933
<v Tutor>Here's how to quote it in an answer.

282
00:21:57.933 --> 00:22:13.700
<v Tutor>HACA in 2002 showed improved neurological outcome with 32 to 34 degrees for 24 hours after witnessed VF arrest, number needed to treat six, but its uncontrolled-fever control arm means the benefit is now attributed largely to fever avoidance, as TTM and TTM2 showed.

283
00:22:14.100 --> 00:22:18.597
<v Tutor>Second, PARAMEDIC2, Perkins and colleagues, New England Journal, 2018.

284
00:22:18.597 --> 00:22:26.495
<v Tutor>This is the trial behind chapter twenty-three of Controversies in Critical Care, on adrenaline and outcomes after cardiac arrest.

285
00:22:26.495 --> 00:22:29.723
<v Tutor>Before I give you the result, try to predict it.

286
00:22:29.723 --> 00:22:32.663
<v Tutor>Did adrenaline improve survival at 30 days?

287
00:22:32.663 --> 00:22:36.641
<v Tutor>And did it improve survival with a good neurological outcome?

288
00:22:36.641 --> 00:22:38.140
<v Tutor>Pause, and answer.

289
00:22:43.540 --> 00:22:45.242
<v Tutor>Here's what happened.

290
00:22:45.242 --> 00:22:57.511
<v Tutor>This was a blinded, placebo-controlled trial in UK ambulance services: 8,014 adults with out-of-hospital cardiac arrest, randomised to 1 milligram of adrenaline every 3 to 5 minutes, or saline placebo.

291
00:22:57.511 --> 00:23:01.796
<v Tutor>The primary outcome, 30-day survival, was 3.2 versus 2.4 percent.

292
00:23:01.796 --> 00:23:03.733
<v Tutor>Adjusted odds ratio 1.47.

293
00:23:03.733 --> 00:23:07.549
<v Tutor>That's about eight extra survivors per thousand patients.

294
00:23:07.549 --> 00:23:17.528
<v Tutor>But survival with a favourable neurological outcome, a modified Rankin score of three or less, at discharge, was 2.2 versus 1.9 percent, which wasn't significant.

295
00:23:17.528 --> 00:23:23.633
<v Tutor>And there was a signal that more of the adrenaline survivors had severe neurological impairment.

296
00:23:23.633 --> 00:23:24.983
<v Tutor>The criticisms.

297
00:23:24.983 --> 00:23:27.977
<v Tutor>It wasn't powered for neurological outcome.

298
00:23:27.977 --> 00:23:32.966
<v Tutor>Survival was lower than expected, so the power calculation had to be revised.

299
00:23:32.966 --> 00:23:37.956
<v Tutor>About one in five patients in each arm was lost to follow-up at three months.

300
00:23:37.956 --> 00:23:43.709
<v Tutor>It doesn't apply to in-hospital arrest, and post-arrest hospital care wasn't standardised.

301
00:23:43.709 --> 00:23:45.763
<v Tutor>And the result was fragile.

302
00:23:45.763 --> 00:23:51.340
<v Tutor>A handful of events in the other direction would have removed statistical significance.

303
00:23:51.520 --> 00:24:00.461
<v Tutor>A Bayesian reanalysis gave only about a 37 percent probability that adrenaline improves survival by at least one percentage point in absolute terms.

304
00:24:00.461 --> 00:24:02.581
<v Tutor>Why might adrenaline do this?

305
00:24:02.581 --> 00:24:06.994
<v Tutor>It raises coronary perfusion pressure, which helps the heart restart.

306
00:24:06.994 --> 00:24:16.451
<v Tutor>But its alpha-agonist effect constricts the cerebral microcirculation, and its beta effects raise myocardial oxygen demand and promote arrhythmia after ROSC.

307
00:24:16.451 --> 00:24:20.406
<v Tutor>So you can restart more hearts while protecting fewer brains.

308
00:24:20.406 --> 00:24:23.271
<v Tutor>That's the mechanism behind the trade-off.

309
00:24:23.271 --> 00:24:36.568
<v Tutor>What it changed: adrenaline stayed in guidelines because it increases survival, but the debate is now about timing and dose, and the uncomfortable trade-off between more survivors and more survivors with severe brain injury.

310
00:24:36.568 --> 00:24:47.400
<v Tutor>How to quote it: PARAMEDIC2 showed adrenaline improved 30-day survival, 3.2 versus 2.4 percent, without a significant improvement in survival with a favourable neurological outcome.

311
00:24:47.800 --> 00:24:52.097
<v Tutor>Third, BOX, Kjaergaard and colleagues, New England Journal, 2022.

312
00:24:52.097 --> 00:24:56.629
<v Tutor>It's the trial behind the blood pressure number in today's guideline.

313
00:24:56.629 --> 00:25:05.223
<v Tutor>BOX was a double-blind, two-by-two factorial trial in Denmark: 789 comatose adults after out-of-hospital arrest of presumed cardiac cause.

314
00:25:05.223 --> 00:25:13.522
<v Tutor>They were randomised to a mean arterial pressure target of 77 versus 63 millimetres of mercury, and separately to two oxygen targets.

315
00:25:13.522 --> 00:25:23.587
<v Tutor>The blinding was clever: the arterial pressure monitors were modified to display a value offset by ten percent, so clinicians couldn't tell which arm they were in.

316
00:25:23.587 --> 00:25:30.650
<v Tutor>The primary outcome, death or discharge with severe disability or coma within 90 days, was 34 versus 32 percent.

317
00:25:30.650 --> 00:25:33.181
<v Tutor>Hazard ratio 1.08, not significant.

318
00:25:33.181 --> 00:25:36.360
<v Tutor>Ninety-day mortality was 31 versus 29 percent.

319
00:25:36.360 --> 00:25:42.835
<v Tutor>Median neuron-specific enolase at 48 hours was similar, and there was no difference in adverse events.

320
00:25:42.835 --> 00:25:46.660
<v Tutor>The oxygen comparison in the same trial was also neutral.

321
00:25:46.660 --> 00:25:49.662
<v Tutor>Why was a higher pressure expected to help?

322
00:25:49.662 --> 00:25:59.080
<v Tutor>After arrest, cerebral autoregulation is often impaired, and the lower limit can be shifted upwards, particularly in patients with chronic hypertension.

323
00:25:59.260 --> 00:26:05.565
<v Tutor>So the theory was that a higher pressure would push more blood through a brain that can't defend its own flow.

324
00:26:05.565 --> 00:26:08.558
<v Tutor>BOX tested that, and on average, it didn't help.

325
00:26:08.558 --> 00:26:09.787
<v Tutor>The criticisms.

326
00:26:09.787 --> 00:26:15.932
<v Tutor>The population was mostly shockable rhythms in a high-performing system, with a high rate of bystander CPR.

327
00:26:15.932 --> 00:26:18.230
<v Tutor>The pressure separation was modest.

328
00:26:18.230 --> 00:26:22.505
<v Tutor>And it can't tell you about patients in cardiogenic shock who need more.

329
00:26:22.505 --> 00:26:29.986
<v Tutor>What it changed: it supports the 2025 target of a mean arterial pressure above 60 to 65, with no proven benefit from pushing higher.

330
00:26:29.986 --> 00:26:40.780
<v Tutor>How to quote it: BOX found no difference in death or severe disability between mean arterial pressure targets of 77 and 63 millimetres of mercury after cardiac arrest, supporting a target of 65.

331
00:26:42.180 --> 00:26:45.828
<v Examiner>This is from the 2024 first sitting, question twenty-eight.

332
00:26:45.828 --> 00:26:46.808
<v Examiner>Ten marks.

333
00:26:46.808 --> 00:26:48.387
<v Examiner>I'll read it exactly.

334
00:26:48.387 --> 00:26:58.298
<v Examiner>Discuss your approach to prognostication of neurological outcome in adult ICU patients after successful cardiovascular resuscitation following out of hospital cardiac arrest.

335
00:26:58.298 --> 00:27:01.619
<v Examiner>Your answer should include the following headings: a.

336
00:27:01.619 --> 00:27:02.654
<v Examiner>Guidelines.

337
00:27:02.654 --> 00:27:03.634
<v Examiner>Two marks.

338
00:27:03.634 --> 00:27:04.178
<v Examiner>b.

339
00:27:04.178 --> 00:27:06.683
<v Examiner>General approach and clinical factors.

340
00:27:06.683 --> 00:27:07.772
<v Examiner>Three marks.

341
00:27:07.772 --> 00:27:08.317
<v Examiner>c.

342
00:27:08.317 --> 00:27:11.475
<v Examiner>Investigations, and their role in prognostication.

343
00:27:11.475 --> 00:27:12.510
<v Examiner>Five marks.

344
00:27:12.510 --> 00:27:14.089
<v Examiner>You have ten minutes.

345
00:27:14.089 --> 00:27:15.940
<v Examiner>Start with the guidelines.

346
00:27:16.440 --> 00:27:23.379
<v Candidate>For guidelines, I'd use the European Resuscitation Council and ESICM post-resuscitation care guideline.

347
00:27:23.379 --> 00:27:27.630
<v Candidate>They recommend a multimodal approach at 72 hours after ROSC.

348
00:27:27.630 --> 00:27:41.634
<v Candidate>Poor outcome is likely if two or more predictors are present, such as absent pupillary and corneal reflexes, absent N20s, a malignant EEG, high neuron-specific enolase, status myoclonus, or diffuse injury on imaging.

349
00:27:41.634 --> 00:27:45.760
<v Candidate>I'd also mention the Neurocritical Care Society guideline.

350
00:27:46.260 --> 00:27:49.540
<v Examiner>Which edition, and what's the entry criterion?

351
00:27:50.040 --> 00:27:53.986
<v Candidate>The 2025 edition, published last year in Resuscitation.

352
00:27:53.986 --> 00:28:02.127
<v Candidate>Entry is a patient who isn't obeying commands, a motor score below six, at 72 hours or later, after excluding confounders.

353
00:28:02.127 --> 00:28:05.948
<v Candidate>The 2021 version used a motor score of three or less.

354
00:28:05.948 --> 00:28:10.081
<v Candidate>And the Neurocritical Care Society guideline is from 2023.

355
00:28:10.081 --> 00:28:16.720
<v Candidate>It grades the pupillary reflex at 72 hours and bilaterally absent N20s as the reliable predictors.

356
00:28:17.220 --> 00:28:17.765
<v Examiner>Good.

357
00:28:17.765 --> 00:28:19.567
<v Examiner>That's where the second mark lives.

358
00:28:19.567 --> 00:28:22.332
<v Examiner>Candidates who just say there are guidelines don't get it.

359
00:28:22.332 --> 00:28:23.380
<v Examiner>General approach.

360
00:28:23.880 --> 00:28:27.502
<v Candidate>The principles are: multimodal, never a single test.

361
00:28:27.502 --> 00:28:29.676
<v Candidate>Timing at 72 hours or later.

362
00:28:29.676 --> 00:28:31.366
<v Candidate>Exclude confounders.

363
00:28:31.366 --> 00:28:33.238
<v Candidate>Repeat the examination.

364
00:28:33.238 --> 00:28:36.800
<v Candidate>And assess with senior and multidisciplinary input.

365
00:28:36.800 --> 00:28:41.690
<v Candidate>For clinical factors, I'd split them into arrest factors and examination.

366
00:28:41.690 --> 00:28:53.945
<v Candidate>Arrest factors associated with a poor outcome are a non-shockable initial rhythm, an unwitnessed arrest, no bystander CPR, a long time to ROSC, say over twenty minutes, older age and comorbidity.

367
00:28:53.945 --> 00:28:57.447
<v Candidate>But they shouldn't be used alone to prognosticate.

368
00:28:57.447 --> 00:29:02.941
<v Candidate>On examination: the pupillary light reflex, ideally with quantitative pupillometry.

369
00:29:02.941 --> 00:29:04.571
<v Candidate>The corneal reflex.

370
00:29:04.571 --> 00:29:11.151
<v Candidate>The motor response, which has a high false-positive rate, so it's only a trigger for further testing.

371
00:29:11.151 --> 00:29:15.800
<v Candidate>And myoclonus, where status myoclonus within 72 hours is a poor sign.

372
00:29:16.300 --> 00:29:17.834
<v Examiner>You said exclude confounders.

373
00:29:17.834 --> 00:29:18.580
<v Examiner>Name them.

374
00:29:19.080 --> 00:29:21.913
<v Candidate>Sedation and neuromuscular blockade.

375
00:29:21.913 --> 00:29:23.201
<v Candidate>Hypothermia.

376
00:29:23.201 --> 00:29:24.489
<v Candidate>Hypotension.

377
00:29:24.489 --> 00:29:29.448
<v Candidate>Metabolic derangements like hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia and uraemia.

378
00:29:29.448 --> 00:29:30.800
<v Candidate>And seizures.

379
00:29:31.300 --> 00:29:35.820
<v Examiner>Why might sedation still be a confounder two days after you've stopped the propofol and fentanyl?

380
00:29:36.320 --> 00:29:45.232
<v Candidate>Because hypothermia slows drug metabolism, and these patients often have shock liver and acute kidney injury, so the drugs and their metabolites accumulate.

381
00:29:45.232 --> 00:29:50.720
<v Candidate>So I'd wait longer after stopping them, and check drug levels or train-of-four if I'm unsure.

382
00:29:51.220 --> 00:29:51.705
<v Examiner>Good.

383
00:29:51.705 --> 00:29:53.382
<v Examiner>You'd also lost a point on myoclonus.

384
00:29:53.382 --> 00:29:54.500
<v Examiner>What's the trap there?

385
00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:55.800
<v Candidate>Right.

386
00:29:55.800 --> 00:29:57.456
<v Candidate>Lance-Adams syndrome.

387
00:29:57.456 --> 00:30:01.968
<v Candidate>That's action myoclonus as the patient wakes, with preserved cognition.

388
00:30:01.968 --> 00:30:08.480
<v Candidate>So I'd record an EEG during the myoclonus and look at the background, rather than assume it's a poor sign.

389
00:30:08.980 --> 00:30:10.296
<v Examiner>Investigations.

390
00:30:10.296 --> 00:30:11.384
<v Examiner>Five marks.

391
00:30:11.384 --> 00:30:16.020
<v Examiner>For each, the examiners want the finding, the timing, and the limitation.

392
00:30:16.520 --> 00:30:22.540
<v Candidate>I'd lay this out as a table: test, poor-outcome finding and timing, and limitation.

393
00:30:22.540 --> 00:30:23.467
<v Candidate>SSEPs.

394
00:30:23.467 --> 00:30:26.973
<v Candidate>Bilaterally absent N20s at 24 hours or later.

395
00:30:26.973 --> 00:30:32.795
<v Candidate>Very low false-positive rate, and relatively unaffected by sedation and cooling.

396
00:30:32.795 --> 00:30:39.741
<v Candidate>Limitations: low sensitivity, muscle artefact, and it needs expertise and isn't always available.

397
00:30:39.741 --> 00:30:40.535
<v Candidate>EEG.

398
00:30:40.535 --> 00:30:49.400
<v Candidate>A highly malignant pattern after 24 hours: a suppressed background, with or without periodic discharges, or burst suppression.

399
00:30:49.400 --> 00:30:53.701
<v Candidate>A continuous reactive background suggests a good outcome.

400
00:30:53.701 --> 00:30:59.787
<v Candidate>Limitations: sedatives suppress it, reactivity isn't standardised, and readers vary.

401
00:30:59.787 --> 00:31:02.963
<v Candidate>It also detects non-convulsive seizures.

402
00:31:02.963 --> 00:31:05.080
<v Candidate>Neuron-specific enolase.

403
00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:10.108
<v Candidate>Above 60 micrograms per litre at 48 or 72 hours, and a rising trend.

404
00:31:10.108 --> 00:31:12.952
<v Candidate>Limitation: no universal threshold.

405
00:31:12.952 --> 00:31:14.077
<v Candidate>CT brain.

406
00:31:14.077 --> 00:31:19.965
<v Candidate>Loss of grey-white differentiation and diffuse oedema, best after 24 to 48 hours.

407
00:31:19.965 --> 00:31:27.838
<v Candidate>Early CT is often normal and doesn't exclude a poor outcome, but it finds causes like subarachnoid haemorrhage.

408
00:31:27.838 --> 00:31:28.632
<v Candidate>MRI.

409
00:31:28.632 --> 00:31:34.454
<v Candidate>Diffuse restricted diffusion, cortex and deep grey matter, at two to seven days.

410
00:31:34.454 --> 00:31:37.960
<v Candidate>Limitations: transport risk and availability.

411
00:31:38.460 --> 00:31:39.779
<v Examiner>Neuron-specific enolase.

412
00:31:39.779 --> 00:31:41.262
<v Examiner>You've given one limitation.

413
00:31:41.262 --> 00:31:42.540
<v Examiner>What else confounds it?

414
00:31:43.040 --> 00:31:44.154
<v Candidate>Haemolysis.

415
00:31:44.154 --> 00:31:48.611
<v Candidate>Red cells contain enolase, so a haemolysed sample or ECMO raises it.

416
00:31:48.611 --> 00:31:52.657
<v Candidate>And neuroendocrine tumours, including small cell lung cancer.

417
00:31:52.657 --> 00:31:55.120
<v Candidate>So I'd check the haemolysis index.

418
00:31:55.620 --> 00:32:00.900
<v Examiner>And how do you put this together for the family on day three, if one test is poor and the rest are indeterminate?

419
00:32:01.400 --> 00:32:03.820
<v Candidate>Then I wouldn't call it a poor prognosis.

420
00:32:03.820 --> 00:32:05.549
<v Candidate>One predictor isn't enough.

421
00:32:05.549 --> 00:32:10.883
<v Candidate>I'd continue support, repeat the assessment, and allow a longer period of observation for awakening.

422
00:32:10.883 --> 00:32:13.846
<v Candidate>I'd be honest with the family about the uncertainty.

423
00:32:13.846 --> 00:32:18.637
<v Candidate>If two or more concordant predictors develop, the consultant would discuss goals of care.

424
00:32:18.637 --> 00:32:22.440
<v Candidate>That includes considering organ donation according to local practice.

425
00:32:22.940 --> 00:32:24.541
<v Examiner>Let's apply it.

426
00:32:24.541 --> 00:32:27.186
<v Examiner>Day four, 80 hours after ROSC.

427
00:32:27.186 --> 00:32:33.242
<v Examiner>Sedation stopped 48 hours ago, temperature 37.2, sodium 139, creatinine normal.

428
00:32:33.242 --> 00:32:35.748
<v Examiner>Motor response is extension.

429
00:32:35.748 --> 00:32:37.210
<v Examiner>Pupils react.

430
00:32:37.210 --> 00:32:39.229
<v Examiner>Corneals are present.

431
00:32:39.229 --> 00:32:42.152
<v Examiner>SSEPs: N20s present on both sides.

432
00:32:42.152 --> 00:32:49.183
<v Examiner>Neuron-specific enolase 38 micrograms per litre at 72 hours, with a mildly haemolysed sample.

433
00:32:49.183 --> 00:32:53.081
<v Examiner>EEG: continuous background with some reactivity.

434
00:32:53.081 --> 00:32:55.100
<v Examiner>What do you conclude?

435
00:32:55.600 --> 00:32:59.807
<v Candidate>This patient doesn't meet the criteria for a likely poor outcome.

436
00:32:59.807 --> 00:33:07.012
<v Candidate>He enters the algorithm because he isn't obeying commands at more than 72 hours with confounders reasonably excluded.

437
00:33:07.012 --> 00:33:09.951
<v Candidate>But none of the six predictors are present.

438
00:33:09.951 --> 00:33:17.617
<v Candidate>Brainstem reflexes are intact, N20s are present, the enolase is below 60, and the haemolysis would only push it up, not down.

439
00:33:17.617 --> 00:33:23.150
<v Candidate>The EEG isn't malignant, and a continuous reactive background actually favours recovery.

440
00:33:23.150 --> 00:33:26.492
<v Candidate>The extensor posture on its own isn't a predictor.

441
00:33:26.492 --> 00:33:30.930
<v Candidate>So the prognosis is indeterminate, leaning towards possible recovery.

442
00:33:30.930 --> 00:33:40.440
<v Candidate>I'd continue full support, consider an MRI between days two and seven, repeat the examination daily, and tell the family it's too early to conclude anything.

443
00:33:40.940 --> 00:33:41.687
<v Examiner>Correct.

444
00:33:41.687 --> 00:33:47.660
<v Examiner>The most dangerous answer here is to read extension at day four as a bad sign and start a conversation about withdrawal.

445
00:33:47.660 --> 00:33:50.180
<v Examiner>That's the self-fulfilling prophecy in action.

446
00:33:50.580 --> 00:33:51.433
<v Examiner>One more.

447
00:33:51.433 --> 00:33:57.909
<v Examiner>The registrar asks whether to send the patient for a CT brain on the way back from the catheter laboratory, at six hours.

448
00:33:57.909 --> 00:34:00.620
<v Examiner>What's the rationale, and what's the downside?

449
00:34:01.120 --> 00:34:14.239
<v Candidate>The rationale for early CT is to find a cause or a complication: subarachnoid haemorrhage, an intracranial bleed after anticoagulation for the coronary procedure, or traumatic injury from a fall at the time of the arrest.

450
00:34:14.239 --> 00:34:19.395
<v Candidate>Finding a subarachnoid haemorrhage changes management and prognosis straight away.

451
00:34:19.395 --> 00:34:27.015
<v Candidate>A late CT, at 48 hours or more, is for the severity of hypoxic injury: loss of grey-white differentiation and diffuse oedema.

452
00:34:27.015 --> 00:34:34.004
<v Candidate>The downside of an early CT is that it's often normal even with severe hypoxic injury, so it can falsely reassure.

453
00:34:34.004 --> 00:34:39.332
<v Candidate>There's also transport risk in an unstable patient, and it can delay other treatment.

454
00:34:39.332 --> 00:34:52.680
<v Candidate>So I'd do it early if there's a clinical reason to suspect an intracranial cause, like no clear cardiac cause or a headache before collapse, and route it on the way back from the catheter laboratory to save a second transfer.

455
00:34:53.180 --> 00:34:57.326
<v Examiner>That's a reasonable answer to the 2024 second sitting question as well.

456
00:34:57.326 --> 00:35:06.509
<v Examiner>Its examiner comments rewarded candidates who stayed in the clinical context of out-of-hospital arrest and talked about the implications of early versus late scanning.

457
00:35:06.509 --> 00:35:10.183
<v Examiner>They didn't reward generic advantages and disadvantages of CT.

458
00:35:10.183 --> 00:35:11.180
<v Examiner>Last probe.

459
00:35:11.180 --> 00:35:14.486
<v Examiner>The family ask what the chances are if he does wake up.

460
00:35:14.486 --> 00:35:16.060
<v Examiner>What do you tell them?

461
00:35:16.560 --> 00:35:22.235
<v Candidate>That it depends a lot on the severity of the brain injury, which we'll know more about after 72 hours.

462
00:35:22.235 --> 00:35:29.046
<v Candidate>Many survivors who wake up have a good functional outcome, but problems with memory, attention, fatigue and mood are common.

463
00:35:29.046 --> 00:35:31.626
<v Candidate>So we'd plan follow-up and rehabilitation.

464
00:35:31.626 --> 00:35:34.360
<v Candidate>I'd avoid giving a precise number on day one.

465
00:35:34.860 --> 00:35:37.663
<v Examiner>Let me tell you where the marks came from.

466
00:35:37.663 --> 00:35:42.877
<v Examiner>Guidelines: you named both, with years, the entry criterion and the two-or-more rule.

467
00:35:42.877 --> 00:35:47.193
<v Examiner>Full marks once prompted, but you didn't give the year until I asked.

468
00:35:47.193 --> 00:35:53.304
<v Examiner>The rubric rewards citing guidelines with specifics, so state the edition and year in the first line.

469
00:35:53.304 --> 00:35:56.275
<v Examiner>General approach and clinical factors: solid.

470
00:35:56.275 --> 00:36:01.769
<v Examiner>Multimodal, timing, confounders, and the motor score as a trigger rather than a predictor.

